WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.712
Now you will see how a small change in a WHERE
condition

00:00:02.738 --> 00:00:07.750
can change the result The query returns MRN full
name age and

00:00:07.775 --> 00:00:12.300
active status from the patients table The WHERE
clause has two conditions

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:18.000
connected with AND First is_active must be true
Second

00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:22.150
age must be greater than or equal to fifty Because
we use

00:00:22.175 --> 00:00:25.475
AND a row must satisfy both conditions to stay in
the result

00:00:26.362 --> 00:00:30.362
Run the query once with fifty as the age threshold
Look at

00:00:30.388 --> 00:00:34.838
which active patients appear Then change fifty to
sixty and run the

00:00:34.862 --> 00:00:40.275
query again The table itself has not changed We only
changed the

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:45.450
condition used to select rows When the threshold
becomes more restrictive fewer

00:00:45.475 --> 00:00:49.288
rows may qualify This is a useful way to think about
a

00:00:49.312 --> 00:00:53.500
predicate which is another word you may hear for a
condition A

00:00:53.512 --> 00:00:58.200
predicate acts like a gate Rows that satisfy the
condition pass through

00:00:59.075 --> 00:01:02.025
Rows that do not satisfy it are left out of the
result

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:07.062
Also notice that ORDER BY age DESC happens after
filtering conceptually so

00:01:07.088 --> 00:01:12.112
the qualifying patients are displayed from older to
younger Small experiments like

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:17.012
this are valuable When learning SQL change one
number or one operator

00:01:17.038 --> 00:01:21.262
at a time and compare the results It helps you
understand exactly

00:01:21.288 --> 00:01:23.738
which part of the statement is controlling the
output
